---
const pillars = [
  { label: "Guardrails", href: "/#expertise" },
  { label: "Evals", href: "/guides/llm-evaluation" },
  { label: "Human Oversight" },
  { label: "Red Teaming" },
];
---

<section id="responsible-ai" class="py-20 md:py-28 border-b border-border">
  <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6">
    <div class="animate-on-scroll grid md:grid-cols-[220px_1fr] gap-10 md:gap-16">
      <div class="flex md:flex-col gap-4 md:gap-6 items-baseline md:items-start">
        <span class="index-numeral" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div class="section-label">Responsible AI</div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <p class="font-display text-3xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-[1.1] max-w-3xl">
          I build AI systems people can trust — governed, evaluated, and <em class="text-primary italic">safe by design</em>, not by accident.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed max-w-[33rem]">
          "Responsible AI" is usually a slide from a compliance team that never
          shipped a model. For me it's an engineering discipline: guardrails that
          actually constrain outputs, eval suites that catch regressions before
          users do, a human in the loop wherever the cost of a wrong answer is
          real, and adversarial testing before the internet does it for me.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-8 pt-2 border-t border-border">
          {pillars.map((pillar) =>
            pillar.href ? (
              <a href={pillar.href} class="tap-link font-mono text-sm text-primary hover:underline underline-offset-4">
                {pillar.label}
              </a>
            ) : (
              <span class="tap-link font-mono text-sm text-foreground">{pillar.label}</span>
            )
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
